OCGRAPH 1
# THE GAME MODE, as a graph. This is what starts the play session.
#
# Before 6d7ff99 this file could declare `CLASS AN_Rules GameMode` and the very next log line would
# still read "no GameMode class declared -- play session not started": class FLAGS were not inherited
# down the registry chain, so a graph parented to GameMode never actually carried the GameMode bit
# and nothing that looks for one could find it. Kind bits flow at seal time now, so declaring this
# genuinely begins play -- and with it everything gated on AVER_FW_PLAY_PLAYING, physics stepping
# included.
#
# WHAT IT STILL CANNOT DO, stated because a reader will look for it: a graph GameMode cannot name a
# default pawn or controller. The CLASS record understands `mesh=` and `material=` and nothing else,
# so there is no equivalent of [AverGameMode(DefaultPawnClass=..., PlayerControllerClass=...)]. The
# player in this project is PLACED by the map rather than spawned-and-possessed by the mode, which is
# a real difference from the framework's normal Controller-mediated flow, not a stylistic one.
NAME Rules
DESCRIPTION The game mode, as a graph. Starts the play session and keeps a round clock in a VAR.

CLASS AN_Rules GameMode

PARAM deltaTime float

# The round clock the graph owns. Nothing outside it accumulates this -- which is the point: before
# VAR existed, a graph wanting elapsed time needed a C# host to sum deltaTime and hand it back as a
# PARAM every tick, and that host was most of a file that existed for no other reason.
VAR elapsed float 0

NODE dt Param param=deltaTime

ENTRY tick OnTick
NODE tick OnTick

NODE prev GetVar var=elapsed
NODE sum Add
LINK prev.value sum.a
LINK dt.value sum.b

NODE store SetVar var=elapsed
LINK tick.exec store.exec
LINK sum.result store.value

# Read back through its own node after the write, for the reason Target.ocgraph's own comment gives.
NODE report GetVar var=elapsed
OUT report value
